  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">BGD100338- Strengthening forecast based early actions Bangladesh (STEP)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The programme, funded by DG ECHO, delivers a 42-month anticipatory action and disaster risk reduction intervention in Patharghata Upazila (Patharghata and Charduani Unions), Barguna District, Bangladesh, targeting highly vulnerable coastal communities exposed to recurrent cyclones and flooding. Implemented by Plan International Bangladesh in partnership with SAP-BD, within a wider consortium led by ACF, the programme strengthens institutional and community capacity to act early based on forecasts, reducing loss and damage._x000d_
_x000d_
Early Warning and Community Preparedness (~40%): Strengthening community-based early warning systems through engagement of Community-Based Organisations (CBOs), women&#x2019;s groups, and local disaster management committees. Activities include 66 monthly meetings and 36 household-level courtyard sessions, reaching over 1,000 community members, to build understanding of forecasts and promote early action planning._x000d_
_x000d_
Institutional Strengthening and DRM Governance (~30%): Enhancing the capacity of disaster and shelter management committees to coordinate preparedness and response, including resource mobilisation planning across four cyclone shelters, with approximately 70% of required materials secured to improve readiness._x000d_
_x000d_
Forecast-Based Action Systems (~20%): Supporting the development and institutionalisation of forecast-based early action protocols, aligned with national systems and linked to social protection mechanisms._x000d_
_x000d_
Advocacy and Systems Integration (~10%): Engagement with government stakeholders and technical agencies to strengthen coordination, policy frameworks, and national uptake of anticipatory action._x000d_
_x000d_
The programme demonstrates that forecast-based early action reduces cyclone-related loss and damage while strengthening community resilience and local systems.</narrative>
  </description>
